  "whatPeopleCouldNotYetSee": "Semmelweis could show that his method worked without being able to explain why, and this gap let his colleagues treat a strong statistical result as unproven rather than as evidence needing an explanation. It took Pasteur's germ theory to supply the mechanism that made the same evidence persuasive to a wider medical establishment.[2]",
  "turningPoint": "Pasteur's research into microorganisms gave germ theory a scientific mechanism, and Lister's antiseptic surgical results gave it a proven clinical application. Together they turned what had been one rejected doctor's statistical finding into an accepted basis for hospital practice within a few decades.[2][3]",
