  "whatArrived": "Around 1779, Samuel Crompton, a weaver in Bolton, Lancashire, built a spinning machine that combined the moving carriage of Hargreaves's spinning jenny with the rollers of Arkwright's water frame.[1][2] The result, later called the spinning mule, could spin thread both fine and strong, and later versions carried well over a thousand spindles on a single frame.[1]",
  "whatItEntered": "By 1779 spinning was already partly mechanized through the jenny and the water frame, but neither machine alone could produce the fine, strong thread needed for the best muslin cloth, which English manufacturers still imported or paid a premium to obtain.[2][3] Crompton himself had grown up spinning by hand on a jenny and built the mule to fix its shortcomings.[2]",
  "firstPromise": "The mule let a single spinner produce yarn fine and consistent enough to compete with imported muslin thread, at a volume one machine-tender's output could match roughly two hundred hand spinners.[1][3]",
  "firstResistance": "Crompton could not afford the cost of a patent, so once word of the mule's quality spread, he showed the design to local manufacturers under an informal promise of payment for his trouble; manufacturers adopted the design freely and the promised payment came to only a hundred guineas, far short of the machine's value to the industry that used it.[2][4]",
  "whatHadToChange": "Manufacturing shifted toward machines fine enough to make the highest grades of cotton cloth domestically rather than through imports, and mule-spinning became a skilled, better-paid trade of its own within the new mills, distinct from both hand spinning and the coarser output of the jenny or water frame alone.[1][3]",
  "whatPeopleCouldNotYetSee": "Crompton and the manufacturers who first copied his design in the 1780s could not have foreseen how completely the mule would come to dominate English spinning: a national survey found between four and five million mule spindles in use by 1812, a scale that made Crompton's uncompensated invention one of the most widely adopted machines in the country within his own lifetime.[4]",
  "turningPoint": "In 1811-12, after decades of near-universal adoption without payment to its inventor, Crompton petitioned Parliament for recognition, and a parliamentary committee investigated his claim, confirming the scale of the mule's use and awarding him a grant of five thousand pounds in 1812, decades after the machine had already transformed the industry.[4]",
